From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2_codec2: do not poll buffers again after POLLERR

V4L2DevicePoller polls the device for buffers until one is ready, and
arms buffer polling again whenever poll() returns without one. A
mem2mem device returns POLLERR at once, without sleeping, while each of
its queues is not streaming, in the error state or without a queued
buffer. The poll thread then calls poll() again immediately and posts
nothing to the client:

- at every decoder start on a driver that does not report the CAPTURE
  format before the first SOURCE_CHANGE event, from start() until the
  first input buffer is queued: the input queue streams without a
  buffer and the CAPTURE queue does not stream yet;
- whenever no queue is streaming with a buffer queued, for example
  while a drain waits for the first SOURCE_CHANGE event;
- for good after the driver marks the queues as failed
  (vb2_queue_error()). The client is never called, so it does not get
  the -EIO that DQBUF would return, and if no further buffer is queued
  decoding hangs with the poll thread busy.

Do not arm buffer polling again after POLLERR. Post the client's
callback once instead, so that a client with buffers still queued
dequeues them and gets the queue error; without queued buffers the
callback does nothing. Buffer polling resumes with the next
schedulePoll(), which every QBUF calls. Call schedulePoll() after
STREAMON as well, because buffers queued before STREAMON only become
ready then.

Without buffer polling a mem2mem device does not return POLLERR at all,
so POLLERR there means that the device itself failed: an unregistered
video node returns POLLERR | POLLHUP | POLLPRI, which the poller used to
turn into an endless series of event callbacks. Report it through the
error callback instead.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 v4l2/V4L2Device.cpp                           |  8 ++++-
 v4l2/V4L2DevicePoller.cpp                     | 30 ++++++++++++++-----
 v4l2/include/v4l2_codec2/v4l2/V4L2Device.h    |  6 +++-
 .../v4l2_codec2/v4l2/V4L2DevicePoller.h       |  4 ++-
 4 files changed, 38 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/v4l2/V4L2Device.cpp b/v4l2/V4L2Device.cpp
index 3650563..187d3c3 100644
--- a/v4l2/V4L2Device.cpp
+++ b/v4l2/V4L2Device.cpp
@@ -1054,6 +1054,10 @@ bool V4L2Queue::streamon() {
     mIsStreaming = true;
     reportTraceMetrics();
 
+    // Buffers queued before STREAMON become ready for poll() only now. The poller stops polling
+    // buffers after poll() returned POLLERR, so make sure it polls them again.
+    mDevice->schedulePoll();
+
     return true;
 }
 
@@ -1194,7 +1198,8 @@ int V4L2Device::ioctl(int request, void* arg) {
     return HANDLE_EINTR(::ioctl(mDeviceFd.get(), request, arg));
 }
 
-bool V4L2Device::poll(bool pollDevice, bool pollBuffers, bool* eventPending, bool* buffersPending) {
+bool V4L2Device::poll(bool pollDevice, bool pollBuffers, bool* eventPending, bool* buffersPending,
+                      bool* errorPending) {
     struct pollfd pollfds[2];
     nfds_t nfds;
     int pollfd = -1;
@@ -1221,6 +1226,7 @@ bool V4L2Device::poll(bool pollDevice, bool pollBuffers, bool* eventPending, boo
     }
     *eventPending = (pollfd != -1 && pollfds[pollfd].revents & POLLPRI);
     *buffersPending = (pollfd != -1 && pollfds[pollfd].revents & (POLLIN | POLLOUT));
+    *errorPending = (pollfd != -1 && pollfds[pollfd].revents & POLLERR);
     return true;
 }
 
diff --git a/v4l2/V4L2DevicePoller.cpp b/v4l2/V4L2DevicePoller.cpp
index b4add7d..e3a7990 100644
--- a/v4l2/V4L2DevicePoller.cpp
+++ b/v4l2/V4L2DevicePoller.cpp
@@ -123,18 +123,30 @@ void V4L2DevicePoller::devicePollTask() {
 
         bool event_pending = false;
         bool buffers_pending = false;
+        bool error_pending = false;
 
         ALOGV("Polling device.");
         bool poll_buffers = mPollBuffers.exchange(false);
-        if (!mDevice->poll(true, poll_buffers, &event_pending, &buffers_pending)) {
+        if (!mDevice->poll(true, poll_buffers, &event_pending, &buffers_pending,
+                           &error_pending)) {
             ALOGE("An error occurred while polling, calling error callback");
             mClientTaskTunner->PostTask(FROM_HERE, mErrorCallback);
             return;
         }
 
-        if (poll_buffers && !buffers_pending) {
-            // If buffer polling was requested but the buffers are not pending,
-            // then set to poll buffers again in the next iteration.
+        if (error_pending && !poll_buffers) {
+            // A mem2mem device returns POLLERR only for buffer polling. Without it, the device
+            // itself has failed, e.g. its video node was unregistered.
+            ALOGE("Device returned POLLERR, calling error callback");
+            mClientTaskTunner->PostTask(FROM_HERE, mErrorCallback);
+            return;
+        }
+
+        if (poll_buffers && !buffers_pending && !error_pending) {
+            // poll() returned for an event or an interrupt before a buffer was ready: poll buffers
+            // again in the next iteration. POLLERR is not re-armed: the device returns it at once
+            // while no queue can make progress, so polling again would only spin until the client
+            // queues a buffer, and queueing a buffer calls schedulePoll() anyway.
             mPollBuffers.exchange(true);
         }
 
@@ -142,9 +154,13 @@ void V4L2DevicePoller::devicePollTask() {
             ALOGE("Failed to clear interrupting device poll.");
         }
 
-        if (buffers_pending || event_pending) {
-            ALOGV("Poll returned, calling event callback. event_pending=%d buffers_pending=%d",
-                  event_pending, buffers_pending);
+        // POLLERR for buffer polling can also mean that a queue is in the error state. Let the
+        // client service the device once, so that it gets the error when it dequeues its
+        // buffers. If it has no buffers queued, the callback does nothing.
+        if (buffers_pending || event_pending || error_pending) {
+            ALOGV("Poll returned, calling event callback. event_pending=%d buffers_pending=%d "
+                  "error_pending=%d",
+                  event_pending, buffers_pending, error_pending);
             mClientTaskTunner->PostTask(FROM_HERE, base::Bind(mEventCallback, event_pending));
         }
     }
diff --git a/v4l2/include/v4l2_codec2/v4l2/V4L2Device.h b/v4l2/include/v4l2_codec2/v4l2/V4L2Device.h
index 0d56b25..d1d0454 100644
--- a/v4l2/include/v4l2_codec2/v4l2/V4L2Device.h
+++ b/v4l2/include/v4l2_codec2/v4l2/V4L2Device.h
@@ -437,8 +437,12 @@ public:
     //   in this case |*eventPending| will be set to true.
     // - |pollBuffers| is true and |pollDevice| is true and there is new data to
     //   be read from the device; in this case |*buffersPending| will be set to true.
+    // - |pollDevice| is true and the device returned POLLERR; in this case |*errorPending| will be
+    //   set to true. With |pollBuffers|, a mem2mem device returns POLLERR at once when each of its
+    //   queues is not streaming, in the error state or without a queued buffer.
     // Returns false on error, true otherwise. This method should be called from a separate thread.
-    bool poll(bool pollDevice, bool pollBuffers, bool* eventPending, bool* buffersPending);
+    bool poll(bool pollDevice, bool pollBuffers, bool* eventPending, bool* buffersPending,
+              bool* errorPending);
 
     // These methods are used to interrupt the thread sleeping on poll() and force it to return
     // regardless of device state, which is usually when the client is no longer interested in what
diff --git a/v4l2/include/v4l2_codec2/v4l2/V4L2DevicePoller.h b/v4l2/include/v4l2_codec2/v4l2/V4L2DevicePoller.h
index 4842088..7081e15 100644
--- a/v4l2/include/v4l2_codec2/v4l2/V4L2DevicePoller.h
+++ b/v4l2/include/v4l2_codec2/v4l2/V4L2DevicePoller.h
@@ -27,7 +27,9 @@ class V4L2Device;
 //
 // Note that the service callback may also be called when no particular event occurred due to the
 // way poll() works. It is the responsibility of the caller to call SchedulePoll() again if there
-// may still be pending events.
+// may still be pending events. After poll() returned POLLERR for the buffer queues, buffers are
+// not polled again until the next call to SchedulePoll(), which queueing a buffer or starting a
+// queue does.
 class V4L2DevicePoller {
 public:
     // Callback to be called when buffer ready/V4L2 event has potentially been polled. |event| is
